WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.968 --> 00:00:02.100
-Welcome back to the show.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.067
Looking good, bud.
Thanks for coming on.

00:00:04.067 --> 00:00:06.834
I just saw your beautiful
wife backstage.

00:00:06.834 --> 00:00:09.934
And today is the 20th
anniversary of you guys meeting?

00:00:09.934 --> 00:00:12.367
-Yeah, This is the day we met
20 years ago.

00:00:12.367 --> 00:00:13.634
-Isn't that cool?
-Yeah.

00:00:13.634 --> 00:00:15.033
[ Cheers and applause ]
-That's awesome, buddy.

00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:17.601
-Yeah, it's really weird.
Actually, um...

00:00:17.601 --> 00:00:19.834
And the movie, "Air,"
is opening today,

00:00:19.834 --> 00:00:22.701
and we realized that
Michael Jordan wore two numbers

00:00:22.701 --> 00:00:27.934
in his career, 45 and 23,
and the date is 4/5/23.

00:00:27.934 --> 00:00:29.267
-Holy --
-Isn't that crazy?

00:00:29.267 --> 00:00:31.601
-Yes! Come on! That's fun.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:31.601 --> 00:00:33.000
-But much more importantly,

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.033
it's 20 years
since I met Lucy, so...

00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:36.801
-Of course. Yeah. Yeah,
that's much more important.

00:00:36.801 --> 00:00:38.601
How is the fam?
Last time we talked,

00:00:38.601 --> 00:00:40.567
you were moving everyone
from L.A. to New York.

00:00:40.567 --> 00:00:42.234
-Right. Yeah, we did.
We -- We --

00:00:42.234 --> 00:00:43.400
It was the smartest thing
we ever did.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:44.467
We're loving it.
-You're here.

00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:45.834
-Yeah, We're in Brooklyn.
-Yeah! Good.

00:00:45.834 --> 00:00:47.734
-Very, very happy. Yeah, yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:47.734 --> 00:00:50.400
-You're loving it.
-Loving it, Loving it. Yeah.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.133
-Let's talk about
the new movie, "Air."

00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:54.167
I got to see it,
and the reviews are in.

00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:56.367
Everyone loves it.
It is fantastic, buddy.

00:00:56.367 --> 00:00:57.567
-Thank you.
-You're so good in this.

00:00:57.567 --> 00:00:58.868
Everyone's good in it,
by the way.

00:00:58.868 --> 00:00:59.968
-Everybody's great.

00:00:59.968 --> 00:01:02.400
I mean, we got just
a lights-out cast.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:04.100
-You really do.
But everything was good.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.467
The script was tight.
You and Ben Affleck, obviously,

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:08.667
everyone loves
you guys together.

00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:12.300
You're great.
This was 25 years ago.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:13.534
-Oh, yeah.
-Look at this.

00:01:13.534 --> 00:01:15.968
When you won the Oscars
for "Good Will Hunting."

00:01:15.968 --> 00:01:18.133
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:18.133 --> 00:01:20.167
Look at how happy --
Look at this.

00:01:20.167 --> 00:01:22.701
-We don't --
-"Yeaaah!!"

00:01:22.701 --> 00:01:25.467
-We don't look too excited.
-Yeah, I mean, you got to be --

00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:27.467
I mean, what's going through --
Do you remember this?

00:01:27.467 --> 00:01:29.033
-I do.
Well, we didn't --

00:01:29.033 --> 00:01:30.834
The weird thing about that
was that we --

00:01:30.834 --> 00:01:33.367
like, the year before,
we were watching it on TV.

00:01:33.367 --> 00:01:37.033
It was like, we had no --
there was no adjustment period

00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:38.400
where we went --
Like, we just suddenly

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.133
were kind of there
in the front row.

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:42.501
And Billy Crystal sang a song
with our names in it.

00:01:42.501 --> 00:01:43.767
We were like,
"What is happening?"

00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:45.100
-"What is going on
with our lives?"

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:48.067
-Yeah. And and we hadn't --
we hadn't talked about

00:01:48.067 --> 00:01:49.634
like, a speech
or anything like that

00:01:49.634 --> 00:01:53.300
because I think as kind
of lifelong Red Sox fans,

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:55.267
you know, you don't
want to jinx anything, so...

00:01:55.267 --> 00:01:57.534
-Yeah. Yeah, exactly.
-Because I think both of us,

00:01:57.534 --> 00:01:58.734
without even talking about it,

00:01:58.734 --> 00:01:59.767
we were like --

00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:01.267
'Cause later we talked about it,

00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:04.334
and it was like, I had this
feeling, like, if I ever said,

00:02:04.334 --> 00:02:06.367
"Hey, we should think
about a speech,"

00:02:06.367 --> 00:02:09.267
I would just flash-forward
to being like 50 years later

00:02:09.267 --> 00:02:10.467
in a bar with him going,

00:02:10.467 --> 00:02:12.167
"Can you believe
we practiced a speech?"

00:02:12.167 --> 00:02:14.234
-Yeah, yeah, yeah!
"That's why we lost."

00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:15.534
-That's why.
-"Because we practiced.

00:02:15.534 --> 00:02:16.868
-"Like we would ever win
an Oscar."

00:02:16.868 --> 00:02:18.334
You know what I mean?
And then suddenly,

00:02:18.334 --> 00:02:22.434
we were up there, and I just
pushed him into the microphone.

00:02:22.434 --> 00:02:23.634
So he --
And he --

00:02:23.634 --> 00:02:26.667
And he just started
kind of babbling and --

00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:29.734
and then saying names
of people who had helped us.

00:02:29.734 --> 00:02:30.968
And then I was next to him,

00:02:30.968 --> 00:02:32.901
and I was just
screaming the names.

00:02:32.901 --> 00:02:35.501
So he would say, like,
Chris Moore.

00:02:35.501 --> 00:02:38.634
And I'd go, "Chris Moore!"
[ Laughter ]

00:02:38.634 --> 00:02:40.934
And he'd go, "John Gordon."
I'd go, "John Gordon!"

00:02:40.934 --> 00:02:42.968
-"John Gordon! Gordon!
Gordie! Gordie!"

00:02:42.968 --> 00:02:44.868
-I was just so happy,
and, like --

00:02:44.868 --> 00:02:47.434
But that was -- that was
my contribution to the speech.

00:02:47.434 --> 00:02:48.734
[ Laughter ]
-Isn't that fun?

00:02:48.734 --> 00:02:51.167
-I was like the hype man
in the corner, you know?

00:02:51.167 --> 00:02:53.801
-Do you still have
that passion and that --

00:02:53.801 --> 00:02:56.267
that love of the movies
and the industry?

00:02:56.267 --> 00:02:57.734
-Yeah. Yeah, absolutely.

00:02:57.734 --> 00:02:59.434
I mean, well, that's one of the
reasons we started this company.

00:02:59.434 --> 00:03:02.033
Ben and I started this kind
of mini studio together,

00:03:02.033 --> 00:03:04.267
and this was our first movie
for the studio,

00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:07.300
which was really cool.
-What's it called again?

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.167
Artists Equity.
-Artists Equity, yeah.

00:03:09.167 --> 00:03:10.534
-Yeah. 'Cause we're pulling
a lot of the crew members.

00:03:10.534 --> 00:03:11.801
We're trying to pull them
above the line

00:03:11.801 --> 00:03:13.167
and get them paid better.

00:03:13.167 --> 00:03:15.767
And -- And, you know,
we kind of feel like --

00:03:15.767 --> 00:03:16.901
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Yeah.

00:03:16.901 --> 00:03:18.167
-Yeah.
-Good for you.

00:03:18.167 --> 00:03:20.701
-So...
-I read about that.

00:03:20.701 --> 00:03:23.200
-But it's definitely,
like, a white-knuckle exercise

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:25.567
because we're responsible
for the overages, you know?

00:03:25.567 --> 00:03:27.434
So it's like...
[ Laughter ]

00:03:27.434 --> 00:03:29.634
It's like, "Oh!"
-Did you go over budget?

00:03:29.634 --> 00:03:31.067
-In three places
we went over budget.

00:03:31.067 --> 00:03:33.667
We went over budget,
but in all really good places.

00:03:33.667 --> 00:03:35.634
We went over budget
in construction

00:03:35.634 --> 00:03:37.467
because we were moving so fast

00:03:37.467 --> 00:03:39.167
that we had
the construction teams

00:03:39.167 --> 00:03:41.200
working 24 hours a day
to build the sets

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.534
or else we were gonna
run out of stuff to shoot.

00:03:43.534 --> 00:03:47.100
So we had budgeted 33 days
and we shot this in 24.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:51.100
So we were moving really fast
without even trying to.

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:54.267
And then we went over
with our -- we had budgeted --

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:56.267
we had allotted a certain
amount of money for cast,

00:03:56.267 --> 00:03:58.701
and then we got this cast,
so we paid more.

00:03:58.701 --> 00:04:00.434
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:00.434 --> 00:04:02.000
-And we were very happy
to pay more.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.734
And then we went
over budget on food trucks.

00:04:05.734 --> 00:04:08.501
[ Laughter ]
Because we had food trucks.

00:04:08.501 --> 00:04:10.467
-You're a smart guy.
You know how to throw a party.

00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:12.467
-Well, we've been doing this
for a long time

00:04:12.467 --> 00:04:13.734
and we know what matters.

00:04:13.734 --> 00:04:15.167
And, like, you know,
people are coming out,

00:04:15.167 --> 00:04:16.934
and they'd be like, "There's a
Korean barbecue truck."

00:04:16.934 --> 00:04:18.234
-Yeah!
That's exactly right!

00:04:18.234 --> 00:04:19.567
Dude, it makes everyone happy.

00:04:19.567 --> 00:04:21.033
-Yeah, it was really, really --

00:04:21.033 --> 00:04:24.200
It was the most fun either of us
have ever had making a movie,

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:25.801
and we've had a lot of fun
making movies.

00:04:25.801 --> 00:04:28.400
-I said it before,
but I really mean it.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.767
You're one of the best actors
to do it.

00:04:30.767 --> 00:04:33.501
And I love watching you act.
It's amazing. You're great.

00:04:33.501 --> 00:04:36.200
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:38.200
I think that you've --
For me, like,

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:40.601
if it was a team,
you're my go-to player.

00:04:40.601 --> 00:04:42.934
If you're on my team, I go,
"Just give the ball to Matt.

00:04:42.934 --> 00:04:44.634
He'll -- He'll nail the line."
You go,

00:04:44.634 --> 00:04:46.934
"How do you like them apples,"
or, you know,

00:04:46.934 --> 00:04:50.067
"Ford v. Ferrari," you're like,
you know, the stopwatch thing.

00:04:50.067 --> 00:04:51.801
You're like, "You want one?
They're Italian."

00:04:51.801 --> 00:04:54.367
It's like, "Yes!"
You have the way of delivering

00:04:54.367 --> 00:04:57.567
these lines that, like,
the crowd goes nuts.

00:04:57.567 --> 00:04:59.133
When I watched "Air,"
you had a couple of them.

00:04:59.133 --> 00:05:02.133
Everyone just claps. Like,
do you go into a script going,

00:05:02.133 --> 00:05:04.434
"Oh, that line will be my --
that'll be the line I nail"?

00:05:04.434 --> 00:05:05.801
-No, no, no.

00:05:05.801 --> 00:05:07.167
-You just read it
and just do it.

00:05:07.167 --> 00:05:08.534
-Yeah, yeah.

00:05:08.534 --> 00:05:12.501
And I mean, you know, I --
I look at the whole script,

00:05:12.501 --> 00:05:13.901
and usually
if it's a good script,

00:05:13.901 --> 00:05:15.067
then it'll have lines in it.

00:05:15.067 --> 00:05:17.100
But I won't kind of plan
it out like that.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:19.067
I mean, "How do you like them
apples" was kind of a very --

00:05:19.067 --> 00:05:22.067
'cause I'm slamming the number
against the window and all that.

00:05:22.067 --> 00:05:24.400
So that definitely
felt like a bigger thing

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:25.834
because it's a bigger thing
in the movie.

00:05:25.834 --> 00:05:28.734
But not -- I didn't think
it would, like, sustain.

00:05:28.734 --> 00:05:30.300
-Do people --
Is that the line

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.267
that everyone yells to you
when they see you?

00:05:32.267 --> 00:05:35.534
-That or they just say my name

00:05:35.534 --> 00:05:37.167
like the puppet
in "Team America."

00:05:37.167 --> 00:05:39.133
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, that's the other way

00:05:39.133 --> 00:05:40.434
to do it, too, yeah.

00:05:40.434 --> 00:05:41.767
-Yeah, that's the other one
I hear a lot. Yeah.

00:05:41.767 --> 00:05:43.968
-Well, when you --
when you get this idea,

00:05:43.968 --> 00:05:46.200
you go, "Okay.
This is a fantastic idea."

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:48.934
This is how kind of
the industry changed, you know?

00:05:48.934 --> 00:05:52.234
And a sneaker company
started working with one person.

00:05:52.234 --> 00:05:54.267
You created
the Air Jordan brand.

00:05:54.267 --> 00:05:56.868
You go, "Okay. First steps.

00:05:56.868 --> 00:05:58.367
We have to talk
to Michael Jordan."

00:05:58.367 --> 00:05:59.734
-Yeah.
-I'm assuming.

00:05:59.734 --> 00:06:01.000
-Yeah, that was
the first thing.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:02.133
Well, because
the script was great --

00:06:02.133 --> 00:06:03.300
-Can't make it without him.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:05.634
-Well, he's not in the movie.
-No.

00:06:05.634 --> 00:06:07.367
-You do realize,
he's not in the movie.

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:10.067
And that's all by design
because it's really about --

00:06:10.067 --> 00:06:12.801
it's about that deal
to make this shoe

00:06:12.801 --> 00:06:15.400
and going back to 1984,

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:17.734
and Nike was not Nike,
like the Nike we know.

00:06:17.734 --> 00:06:20.100
They were like this underdog.
They were in third place

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:22.801
in the basketball division.
-That already blew me away.

00:06:22.801 --> 00:06:24.167
When that came up, I'm like,
"Oh, yeah."

00:06:24.167 --> 00:06:26.133
It was Converse and then --

00:06:26.133 --> 00:06:28.634
-Converse, then Adidas,
and then Nike.

00:06:28.634 --> 00:06:31.234
-Yeah. And I -- I mean,
I didn't -- I read the script,

00:06:31.234 --> 00:06:33.267
and I was, like, blown away.
I was like, "This is all true?"

00:06:33.267 --> 00:06:36.000
-Yeah,
-But so that's -- I think,

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:37.534
you know, Alex,
the screenwriter,

00:06:37.534 --> 00:06:40.534
did a really good job
of kind of setting

00:06:40.534 --> 00:06:42.701
what things
looked like in 1984.

00:06:42.701 --> 00:06:45.367
And it just was
really surprising.

00:06:45.367 --> 00:06:47.467
-So, did you have a meeting
with Michael,

00:06:47.467 --> 00:06:48.834
or did you make Ben do that?

00:06:48.834 --> 00:06:50.501
-I made Ben do that.
-Yeah, smart.

00:06:50.501 --> 00:06:52.634
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
"Ben, Come on. Charm him," yeah.

00:06:52.634 --> 00:06:54.100
-No, Ben went down
to talk to him,

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:58.234
and actually, that was kind of
the critical thing because --

00:06:58.234 --> 00:07:00.901
because we,
out of that meeting, Michael --

00:07:00.901 --> 00:07:02.200
Michael --
Because first of all,

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:03.767
we said, like, "Look,
we're not gonna do it.

00:07:03.767 --> 00:07:05.601
If you don't want us to do it,
we're not gonna do it."

00:07:05.601 --> 00:07:08.501
And he was like, "No, no,
it's a good idea for a movie."

00:07:08.501 --> 00:07:10.434
But Ben said, "What is --
What's most important

00:07:10.434 --> 00:07:11.567
that we get right?

00:07:11.567 --> 00:07:12.734
Like, what's most
important to you?"

00:07:12.734 --> 00:07:14.334
And what he spoke about
were three things.

00:07:14.334 --> 00:07:17.300
There was Howard White,
who's an executive

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:19.167
that Chris Tucker
plays in the movie.

00:07:19.167 --> 00:07:20.334
-Oh, he's great.
-Yeah, he's awesome.

00:07:20.334 --> 00:07:24.734
And George Raveling,
who Marlon Wayans plays.

00:07:24.734 --> 00:07:26.000
George Raveling,
was the first person

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:28.367
who talked to Michael
about Nike,

00:07:28.367 --> 00:07:32.000
and then he talked at length
about his mother.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:34.701
And that was kind of
the critical thing.

00:07:34.701 --> 00:07:36.701
It was like,
"Oh, wait a minute."

00:07:36.701 --> 00:07:38.334
You know -- You know,
to see, like, Michael,

00:07:38.334 --> 00:07:40.767
who's very intimidating dude,

00:07:40.767 --> 00:07:43.300
you know, suddenly
kind of be awestruck

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:44.434
and talk about his mom.

00:07:44.434 --> 00:07:48.133
And so that was --
so Ben said like,

00:07:48.133 --> 00:07:50.467
"Look, who do you think
we should get to play --"

00:07:50.467 --> 00:07:53.100
and before he finished
the sentence, the question,

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:55.367
Michael was like, "Viola Davis."
-Yeah.

00:07:55.367 --> 00:07:57.601
So, Ben -- yeah, yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:57.601 --> 00:07:59.501
-I mean, Viola Davis.

00:07:59.501 --> 00:08:01.434
-That's who you go to,
to hit the shot, by the way.

00:08:01.434 --> 00:08:02.567
-Yeah, that's who you go to.

00:08:02.567 --> 00:08:03.701
Yeah, you give it
to Viola Davis.

00:08:03.701 --> 00:08:05.033
-So he called me.

00:08:05.033 --> 00:08:07.033
He was like,
"I got great news and bad news."

00:08:07.033 --> 00:08:08.067
And I'm like, "Okay."
And he's like,

00:08:08.067 --> 00:08:09.334
"The great news is, you know,

00:08:09.334 --> 00:08:11.767
I know exactly what to do
for the rewrite.

00:08:11.767 --> 00:08:13.367
Like, we're gonna
put in George Raveling,

00:08:13.367 --> 00:08:14.868
we're gonna put in Howard White,

00:08:14.868 --> 00:08:19.334
and we're gonna make his mom
central to the whole thing.

00:08:19.334 --> 00:08:20.767
And I go, "Great.
What's the bad news?"

00:08:20.767 --> 00:08:22.300
He goes,
"We have to get Viola Davis

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:23.968
or we can't make the movie."
You were like,

00:08:23.968 --> 00:08:25.234
"All right, all right.
That is our mission."

00:08:25.234 --> 00:08:26.868
Because everyone wants
Viola Davis in there.

00:08:26.868 --> 00:08:28.734
-That's what I said to Ben.
I was like, that's like saying

00:08:28.734 --> 00:08:30.400
you want Michael Jordan
on your basketball team.

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:32.334
-Yeah, exactly!
It's a good way to put it.

00:08:32.334 --> 00:08:35.267
Yeah.
-So we went, and we rewrote,

00:08:35.267 --> 00:08:39.200
and we really just
kind of drilled down on that --

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:42.133
on that character
and the dynamic between her

00:08:42.133 --> 00:08:44.133
and everybody else in the movie.
-She was phenomenal.

00:08:44.133 --> 00:08:46.267
-She's phenomenal.
-She's great.

00:08:46.267 --> 00:08:50.133
And it's so well done,
well-acted, well-written.

00:08:50.133 --> 00:08:52.534
Everything was good.
It's funny, as well, by the way.

00:08:52.534 --> 00:08:54.634
-Yeah.
It doesn't seem like something

00:08:54.634 --> 00:08:55.868
that would be a great movie.

00:08:55.868 --> 00:08:58.000
It's like,
"It's about a shoe deal?"

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:00.834
-Yeah, yeah.
-But trust me, it's really --

00:09:00.834 --> 00:09:02.667
It's great.
It's a lot of fun.

00:09:02.667 --> 00:09:03.767
-By the end --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:03.767 --> 00:09:05.667
-Yeah.
-No, it makes you feel good.

00:09:05.667 --> 00:09:06.968
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:09:06.968 --> 00:09:08.801
That's what we used to call
a feel-good movie, right?

00:09:08.801 --> 00:09:10.634
-A feel-good movie of the year.
Yeah.

00:09:10.634 --> 00:09:13.267
And it's like, at the end,
you're like, standing ovation,

00:09:13.267 --> 00:09:15.634
clapping, like, "Yes!
I'm so happy for everybody."

00:09:15.634 --> 00:09:18.267
It's wild. You're getting
fantastic reviews.

00:09:18.267 --> 00:09:20.601
A lot of critics are saying this
is the best movie of the year

00:09:20.601 --> 00:09:22.601
so far, so congratulations.
-It's only April.

00:09:22.601 --> 00:09:24.701
-That's true. Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:24.701 --> 00:09:26.534
-We haven't seen
"Super Mario Bros." yet.

00:09:26.534 --> 00:09:27.667
-That's true. Yeah, yeah.

00:09:27.667 --> 00:09:28.968
We have to wait for this
weekend.

00:09:28.968 --> 00:09:30.167
I want to show a clip.

00:09:30.167 --> 00:09:32.467
Here's Matt Damon
and Viola Davis in "Air."

00:09:32.467 --> 00:09:34.167
Take a look.

00:09:34.167 --> 00:09:35.667
-Are you Mr. Jordan?

00:09:35.667 --> 00:09:38.267
-James.
-Sonny Vaccaro. I'm with Nike.

00:09:38.267 --> 00:09:40.934
-[ Laughing ] Oh, man.
Here we go.

00:09:40.934 --> 00:09:43.367
-Well --
-James?

00:09:43.367 --> 00:09:46.300
Who is it?
-Hello, Missus -- Mrs. Jordan.

00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:48.534
My name is Sonny Vaccaro.
I-I'm with Nike.

00:09:48.534 --> 00:09:50.234
-I believe Mr. Faulk
made it clear

00:09:50.234 --> 00:09:52.133
that we weren't interested.

00:09:52.133 --> 00:09:54.534
-Yes, I-I was told not to call.

00:09:54.534 --> 00:09:56.334
-Do you typically make it
a habit of showing up

00:09:56.334 --> 00:09:58.334
at people's front doors
unannounced?

00:09:58.334 --> 00:10:00.300
-Well, maybe the worst
of all my faults,

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:01.667
and believe me, there are many,

00:10:01.667 --> 00:10:04.834
But I don't like
to take no for an answer.

00:10:04.834 --> 00:10:07.601
And I actually think your son
should be endorsed by someone

00:10:07.601 --> 00:10:09.400
with that exact mind-set.

00:10:16.133 --> 00:10:18.934
-All right.
Come on back. Take a seat.

00:10:18.934 --> 00:10:21.567
-Thank you.

00:10:21.567 --> 00:10:23.200
-Yeah. Come on, bud.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:24.501
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:24.501 --> 00:10:28.834
Matt Damon, everybody!
"Air" is in theaters now.

00:10:28.834 --> 00:10:31.434
Matt and I are playing
Beer Pong Basketball

00:10:31.434 --> 00:10:33.133
when we come back.
Stick around.

